# Disposition Effect

The disposition effect is a market anomaly in which investors tend to hold losing investments too long and sell winning investments too early. This behavior is inconsistent with rational portfolio management and contributes to persistent market inefficiencies.

## Why It Happens

- Investors are loss-averse (connecting to [[prospect-theory]])
- Selling winners provides a sense of accomplishment and capitalizes gains
- Holding losers allows avoidance of realizing a loss
- Mental accounting treats gains and losses in separate accounts

## Evidence

Thaler documents how the disposition effect leads to excessive trading, higher transaction costs, and suboptimal portfolio performance. It connects to [[mental-accounting]]—people evaluate investments relative to their purchase price (reference point) rather than based on future expected value.

## Implications

Recognizing the disposition effect helps investors make more rational sell decisions based on future prospects rather than past purchase prices.